WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.880
"Free Guy" stars Ryan Reynolds, contains big&nbsp;
laughs, and is the movie COVID-19 bumped so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:12.400
times that it became a running joke. But there&nbsp;
might actually be an undercurrent of existential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.440
dread buried beneath this flick's feel-good&nbsp;
surface. These are the biggest unanswered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.960
questions in "Free Guy." Caution: spoilers ahead!

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:26.240
Isaac Asimov's Three Laws of Robotics dictate&nbsp;
that robots may not harm a human being,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.360
must obey orders unless they&nbsp;
conflict with the First Law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.400
and must protect their own existence, unless&nbsp;
doing so conflicts with either previous Law.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:39.280
The NPCs of "Free City" do not have&nbsp;
corporeal forms in our world. They do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.320
however, have bodies in the game. They are also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.840
undeniably, computer creations. Since your&nbsp;
average "Free City" NPC has a body created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:53.600
via computer and acts on impulses directed&nbsp;
by a computer, doesn't that make them robots?

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.680
This is an important question, because&nbsp;
if "background" characters do not possess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:02.880
artificial intelligence, their actions would be&nbsp;
severely limited. They wouldn't be able to rebel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:07.520
against Antwan, thereby harming their creator.&nbsp;
They wouldn't be allowed to disobey orders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.280
like the girl in the coffee shop does&nbsp;
when she decides to make a cappuccino.&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:17.440
"Free Guy" is a breezy summer popcorn movie, so it&nbsp;
avoids explicitly asking questions it doesn't want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:23.520
to answer. But if Guy is the only AI-powered&nbsp;
NPC in Free City, the only way to explain the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:28.000
rebellious actions of others in the film's&nbsp;
climax is to suppose that his artificial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.960
intelligence is somehow infectious.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.960
Let's assume for a moment that the&nbsp;
NPCs of Free City aren't robots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:40.080
but living beings who simply don't understand&nbsp;
things like life, death, and free will.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.720
If this is the case, then the film's climax,&nbsp;
in which Antwan destroys the servers that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.600
house the game with an axe, is a tragedy&nbsp;
on par with the destruction of Alderaan.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.720
We witness much of Free City's destruction:&nbsp;
Buildings collapse, streets tear themselves apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:00.560
and hundreds of people flee for their lives. We&nbsp;
are informed that Antwan destroys every server&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:06.720
except the last one, which coincidentally houses&nbsp;
Guy and friends we care about like Buddy. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:12.000
let's assume that he destroys 49 out of 50&nbsp;
servers, which feels like the right amount,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.400
based on the room we see. This means Antwan&nbsp;
kills 98% of the people in Free City.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:24.320
Sure, Antwan is supposed to be the film's villain.&nbsp;
But this makes him a mass murderer who slaughters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:28.800
hundreds, if not thousands of people who&nbsp;
don't even possess the mental capacity to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:34.400
realize their lives are ending. It really takes&nbsp;
the character to a whole new level of evil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:40.240
But hey, Guy and his funny friends live, so&nbsp;
why should we care about those robotic NPCs?

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.400
"I was created as a love

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.840
letter to you," Guy tells Millie at the end of&nbsp;
the film. "You need to go find the author." This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:54.960
is a tremendously sweet line. It's also really,&nbsp;
really messed up. Guy is admitting that every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:00.720
fiber of his being was manufactured to love&nbsp;
one person -- a person forever out of reach.
 &nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.198
"That's her."

00:03:04.198 --> 00:03:05.280
'Who?"

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:06.400
"That's my dream girl, buddy."
 &nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:12.320
Let's assume Millie regularly visits Guy via&nbsp;
avatar. What could be more miserable than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:17.280
watching your soulmate grow old with someone&nbsp;
else? Now, let's assume Millie eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:23.040
stops visiting Guy regularly. What does he do now?&nbsp;
How can he possibly find happiness, knowing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.640
any woman he meets is destined to fall short?
By creating Guy as a love letter, Keys doomed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:33.840
his creation to a life of loneliness. Sure,&nbsp;
Guy looks happy at the end of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:39.280
but if there's a "Free Guy" sequel, the&nbsp;
character's damning mixture of self-awareness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:44.880
and unfulfillable romantic expectations are&nbsp;
likely to plunge him into a deep depression.&nbsp;

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:50.400
Perhaps in a sequel, Millie and Keys could build&nbsp;
a second AI-powered character who looks, acts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.760
and sounds exactly like Millie, allowing Guy to&nbsp;
finally find love. But now we're getting into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:00.800
Alfred Hitchcock "Vertigo" territory. Anyway,&nbsp;
"Free Guy" filmmakers, if you use that idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.600
be sure to send us the royalty checks.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:09.120
In what is undoubtedly the most crowd-pleasing&nbsp;
moment of the film, Guy turns around a violent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.920
smackdown from the newly-uploaded, half-finished&nbsp;
Dude by selecting various weapons added to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:18.320
repertoire. Among other iconic items, we&nbsp;
see Captain America's shield, Hulk's arm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.480
a lightsaber, and a "Fortnite" weapon.
If you go by his facial expressions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:27.520
Guy recognizes these weapons. He also knows how&nbsp;
to use them. But how could Guy know what "Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:33.680
Wars," "Fortnite," and the MCU are? And if he's so&nbsp;
pop-culturally aware, why doesn't he recognize his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.901
own story's resemblances to "They Live"&nbsp;
and "The Lego Movie"?

00:04:37.901 --> 00:04:40.785
"What are these, trick glasses or something?!"

00:04:40.785 --> 00:04:45.760
It seems pretty clear that he's enjoying the connotations of&nbsp;
his weapons as much as their functionality.&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.960
Throughout the film, we never see any indication&nbsp;
that Guy plays video games or watches anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:55.040
other than the daily news report on Free&nbsp;
City's inevitable chaos and destruction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:59.360
It also seems doubtful that anybody would have&nbsp;
spoken to him about these properties, since his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:04.560
peers are equally-clueless NPCs or outsiders who&nbsp;
aren't in the habit of chit-chatting with Guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:06.800
before he becomes a household name.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.760
"Free Guy" is set in two locations: The&nbsp;
virtual Free City, and the non-virtual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.240
unnamed city where Antwan's company is&nbsp;
headquartered. So, why do they both look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:22.080
like Boston? The word "Boston" is never uttered in&nbsp;
the movie, but we do see the Financial District,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.160
the city's distinctive waterfront,&nbsp;
the Boston Harbor Hotel, and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:31.680
instantly-recognizable locales inside the game.
Is the game called "Free City: Boston"?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:37.840
Nope. Is it exactly mapped to Boston?&nbsp;
Nope. But there's enough Boston-specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:42.800
stuff present to make things confusing. Video&nbsp;
games typically either recreate a famous city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:48.000
and use that as a selling point, or construct&nbsp;
something wholly original. This does neither.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:53.440
Perhaps Millie and Keys are Bostonians who modeled&nbsp;
"Free City" after their own environment. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:58.480
everything is referred to in extremely generic&nbsp;
terms, despite the landscape's specificity.
 &nbsp;

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:02.701
"Life in the big city. Ain't nothing like it."

00:06:03.143 --> 00:06:05.876
Of course, "Free Guy" was shot in and around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.876 --> 00:06:09.520
Massachusetts, with lots of important&nbsp;
scenes filmed in Beantown proper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:15.200
But since the locations are unnamed and no attempt&nbsp;
is made to acknowledge Boston's people, culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:20.000
or eccentricities, the audience is left wondering&nbsp;
why the creators of "Free City" bothered to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.880
model their entire game off the Hub.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:27.360
The next time you watch "Free Guy," keep&nbsp;
an eye on Mouser. As his nickname implies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:32.480
he's a sneaky guy -- and he might also be&nbsp;
the Machiavelli behind the entire plot.&nbsp;

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:37.040
Which one was Mouser, you ask? He's Keys'&nbsp;
programmer buddy, ostensibly stuck listening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:41.680
to him talk about his love life. He's also&nbsp;
forced to execute Antwan's evil orders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:46.400
Finally, he assists Millie on the sly,&nbsp;
without any apparent motivation to do so.&nbsp;

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:52.240
This seemingly unimportant character constantly&nbsp;
dispenses advice which Millie, Keys, and Antwan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:58.000
unfailingly act upon. He shuts down the game,&nbsp;
interferes with the game, and adds new elements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.880
to the game, many of which are unsuccessful.
But what if he isn't failing in these moments?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:07.920
What if, instead, he's manipulating everyone&nbsp;
to his own advantage? Look back, and you'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:13.760
realize that if a single character ignored one&nbsp;
of Mouser's pieces of information and/or advice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:19.120
the entire film would have played out differently.&nbsp;
But he's so good at pulling strings that all three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:24.400
characters think he's operating on their behalf.
What's his angle? Well, Mouser leans towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:29.360
Antwan for much of the film, figuring the odds&nbsp;
are in the billionaire's favor. But when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:34.720
balance of power shifts, Mouser becomes a good&nbsp;
guy and lands a sweet gig on Millie and Keys' next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:40.720
project. It's an intriguing subtext for a movie&nbsp;
about avatars manipulated by distant geeks. Mouser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:44.134
might just be the geek driving the entire film.

